\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs22\lang9 Absolutely \f1\emdash  let\rquote s break this down **step by step**, from a single generator all the way to FixMate, and I\rquote ll explain exactly **how a generator \ldblquote connects\rdblquote ** without needing custom hardware. I\rquote ll consolidate everything and make it explicit.\par
\par
---\par
\par
# **FixMate Integration with Generators (No Custom Hardware)**\par
\par
## **1. How a Generator Can \ldblquote Connect\rdblquote **\par
\par
Generators have a **Digital Control Panel (DCP)** or built-in controller that already monitors everything you care about. The connection to FixMate is **purely software** \emdash  either via the cloud, a network protocol, or a serial interface.\par
\par
Think of it like this:\par
\par
```\par
Generator Controller \f2\u8594?\f0  Connection Layer \f2\u8594?\f0  FixMate\par
```\par
\par
### **What the controller monitors**\par
\par
* Engine runtime hours\par
* Fuel level\par
* Oil temperature & pressure\par
* Coolant temperature\par
* Load percentage (kW / kVA)\par
* Battery voltage\par
* Fault codes (overheat, low oil, overspeed)\par
\par
The controller **exposes this data**, which is what FixMate needs.\par
\par
---\par
\par
## **2. Ways to Connect a Generator**\par
\par
### **A. Cloud-Enabled Generators**\par
\par
Modern generators often come with **cloud connectivity**. Examples:\par
\par
| Brand       | Connection Type    | Notes                        |\par
| ----------- | ------------------ | ---------------------------- |\par
| Caterpillar | REST API           | Requires API key, OAuth      |\par
| FG Wilson   | Cloud REST API     | Subscription may be required |\par
| Cummins     | PowerCommand Cloud | REST API endpoints           |\par
| Kohler      | Cloud / Webhooks   | API + webhooks               |\par
\par
**How it works:**\par
\par
1. Generator sends its data to the manufacturer\rquote s cloud.\par
2. You request data from the cloud via **API calls**.\par
3. FixMate receives structured JSON data.\par
\par
**Pros:** Easy setup, no on-site hardware needed.\par
**Cons:** May need subscription, only for supported models.\par
\par
---\par
\par
### **B. Local Network-Enabled Generators**\par
\par
Some generators expose **LAN/Wi-Fi interfaces** and support protocols like:\par
\par
| Protocol   | Example Metrics             | Notes                          |\par
| ---------- | --------------------------- | ------------------------------ |\par
| Modbus TCP | Runtime, load, temperature  | Poll \ldblquote registers\rdblquote  on controller |\par
| SNMP       | Battery voltage, faults     | Poll OIDs                      |\par
| BACnet     | HVAC/industrial integration | Standardized, less common      |\par
\par
**How it works:**\par
\par
1. Connect generator controller to your LAN.\par
2. Poll registers or OIDs using software.\par
3. Convert raw values into FixMate schema.\par
4. Feed telemetry into FixMate DB.\par
\par
**Pros:** Works offline, no cloud needed.\par
**Cons:** Requires network setup and IP access.\par
\par
---\par
\par
### **C. Serial / Local Protocol Generators (Legacy)**\par
\par
Older generators may only support **RS232 or RS485**.\par
\par
**Solution:**\par
\par
* Use a small gateway (e.g., Raspberry Pi) to convert serial \f2\u8594?\f0  TCP/HTTP/MQTT.\par
* Node.js or Python software polls the generator and sends data to FixMate.\par
\par
**Pros:** Works for older models.\par
**Cons:** Requires on-site microcomputer, software setup.\par
\par
---\par
\par
## **3. What FixMate Needs From the Generator**\par
\par
To integrate, you need a **standardized JSON schema**:\par
\par
| Field              | Example               | Purpose                   |\par
| ------------------ | --------------------- | ------------------------- |\par
| engineRuntimeHours | 5423                  | PM scheduling             |\par
| fuelLevelPercent   | 35                    | Low fuel alerts           |\par
| oilTempC           | 88                    | Overheat detection        |\par
| coolantTempC       | 72                    | Critical threshold alerts |\par
| loadPercent        | 60                    | Prevent overload          |\par
| status             | RUNNING/STOPPED/FAULT | Dashboard display         |\par
| faultCodes         | 0x12,0x34             | Trigger maintenance rules |\par
\par
**Important:** Regardless of how the data comes in (cloud, network, serial), you **map it to this structure**.\par
\par
---\par
\par
## **4. Node.js Polling Example (Modbus TCP)**\par
\par
```javascript\par
import ModbusRTU from "modbus-serial";\par
const client = new ModbusRTU();\par
\par
async function pollGenerator() \{\par
  try \{\par
    await client.connectTCP("192.168.1.50", \{ port: 502 \});\par
    await client.setID(1);\par
\par
    // Engine runtime hours\par
    const runtime = await client.readHoldingRegisters(0, 2);\par
    const runtimeHours = runtime.buffer.readUInt32BE();\par
\par
    // Oil temperature\par
    const oilTemp = await client.readHoldingRegisters(2, 1);\par
    const oilTempC = oilTemp.buffer.readUInt16BE();\par
\par
    // Convert to FixMate schema\par
    const telemetry = \{\par
      engineRuntimeHours: runtimeHours,\par
      oilTempC,\par
      status: "RUNNING"\par
    \};\par
\par
    // Send to FixMate API\par
    await fetch("https://api.fixmate.com/generator", \{\par
      method: "POST",\par
      headers: \{ "Content-Type": "application/json" \},\par
      body: JSON.stringify(telemetry),\par
    \});\par
\par
  \} catch (err) \{\par
    console.error("Polling error:", err);\par
  \} finally \{\par
    client.close();\par
  \}\par
\}\par
\par
setInterval(pollGenerator, 60000); // every 60 seconds\par
```\par
\par
**Key points:**\par
\par
* No custom sensors needed.\par
* Software reads what the generator already measures.\par
* Data is standardized and sent to FixMate.\par
\par
---\par
\par
## **5. Step-by-Step Flow**\par
\par
Here\rquote s the entire integration **from generator to FixMate**:\par
\par
```\par
[Generator Controller]\par
        \f3\u9474?\f0\par
        \f3\u9474?\f0  Cloud API / Modbus / SNMP / Serial\par
        \f3\u9660?\f0\par
[Connection Layer / Polling Service]\par
        \f3\u9474?\f0\par
        \f3\u9474?\f0  Transforms data into FixMate JSON schema\par
        \f3\u9660?\f0\par
[FixMate API & Database]\par
        \f3\u9474?\f0\par
        \f3\u9474?\f0  Rules engine triggers tasks/alerts\par
        \f3\u9660?\f0\par
[Dashboard & Notifications]\par
```\par
\par
**Step-by-step for one generator:**\par
\par
1. Identify generator model & connectivity type.\par
2. Decide on **connection method**: Cloud, LAN protocol, or Serial.\par
3. Build a **polling service** in Node.js or Python.\par
4. Map telemetry to FixMate schema.\par
5. POST data to FixMate API every few minutes.\par
6. Define triggers in FixMate (overheat, low fuel, runtime thresholds).\par
7. Dashboard updates automatically.\par
\par
---\par
\par
## \f3\u9989?\f0  Summary\par
\par
* You **don\rquote t need any new hardware**; just use what the generator already provides.\par
* Cloud-enabled generators \f2\u8594?\f0  API\par
* Network-enabled generators \f2\u8594?\f0  Modbus/SNMP/BACnet\par
* Serial generators \f2\u8594?\f0  Serial-to-network gateway\par
* All data is normalized into FixMate\rquote s JSON schema.\par
* Once set up, FixMate can monitor multiple generators, trigger tasks, and provide real-time dashboards.\par
